# A complete sampling scheme for hybrid4

Generated by `studies/rev3/h4_scheme.py`; see its docstring for the derivations.

## I. The curvature simplex S*

Conditions (derivatives in xi = mu^2 on [0, 1]): I(0) >= 0, I' >= 0, I'' <= 0, I''' >= 0.
They are equivalent to five linear inequalities, so S* is a 4-simplex with vertices

| vertex | w1 | w2 | w3 | w4 | profile |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| | -0.08116 | -0.25685 | -0.00059 | +1.33860 | I(0) = 0, pole-dominated (I(mu=0.1) = 0.067, I(0.5) = 0.799) |
| | +0.00063 | +0.00140 | +1.02096 | -0.02299 | I(0) = 0, pole-dominated (I(mu=0.1) = 0.460, I(0.5) = 0.996) |
| | -0.00000 | +1.00000 | -0.00000 | -0.00000 | I = 1 - (1 - mu^2)^2 |
| | +0.50000 | +0.50000 | -0.00000 | -0.00000 | I = mu^2 |
| | -0.00000 | -0.00000 | -0.00000 | -0.00000 | I = 1 (no darkening) |

Volume 0.0285 (w-simplex 1/24 = 0.0417; exact region P = 0.322). Of P, S* holds 9.0% and the w-simplex 13.1%; 18.3% of S* lies outside the w-simplex, and 44.1% of the w-simplex lies outside S* (profiles convex in mu^2, e.g. the vertex I = mu^4).

q-map: 200000 uniform q -> S*: 100.0000% satisfy the exact conditions I-II; round trip q -> w -> q max error 2.1e-13.

